diff --git a/docs/frontends.md b/docs/frontends.md
index bbffbd09411109dd6cb19f7fe63adb7d06eda130..ed6e186a638fa6cc6398d7c7372fdafb0643a144 100644
--- a/docs/frontends.md
+++ b/docs/frontends.md
@@ -123,6 +123,17 @@ The folder a Mac app's save is synced through ([Cloud saves](settings.md#cloud-s
 - **The iPhone app** keeps its folder as a security-scoped bookmark that only its own folder picker can make, so it is chosen there.
 - **The player's choice.** When the cloud's save and this device's both changed since the last sync (or the device has never synced and both have a save), the host takes the newer one, and the other is kept in `<DM_SAVE>-backups`. A front end that asks the player instead sets `DM_SAVE_SYNC_CHOICE` at every start; then the host replaces neither until the player has chosen: it plays with this device's and sends nothing. The front end compares the two before the start with `save_sync_report()` (`dm/host/save_sync.h`: one line of the two saves' hashes, dates, sizes and the cloud's device, found and fetched as the mount will; the Mac and iPhone launchers read it through `SaveSyncReport.swift`), or by itself (the header of `save.dmsync` has the cloud's hash, save time and device; `<DM_SAVE>.sync` has the hash last synced, in a `base` line; `dm/host/save_sync.c` has both formats), and passes what the player chose (a Mac app whose `Info.plist` has the integer `DMSaveSyncChoice=1` reads it): `ask` when there was nothing to choose (the two were the same, or only one changed), `cloud:<hash>` brings in the cloud's save with that hash, `local:<hash>` lets this device's replace it (`local:none` when the cloud had none), and the one not chosen is kept in `<DM_SAVE>-backups`. A choice holds only for the cloud save it names: if that save changed after the check, the choice is not used, and a save that changed on both sides is replaced on neither. `offline` says the cloud could not be read before the start: the host does not wait for it. Pass `ask` only after a comparison: with a choice set and nothing compared, a save that changed on both sides stays on neither side's until a start that asks. With or without a front end, a send never replaces a cloud save that changed after the start compared it, or one the start could not read: the next start decides.
 
+### Per-game running time
+
+Rebuilt Apple runtimes record awake running time, including loading and menus but excluding runtime suspension and sleep. `dm/apple/GameTime` exposes the shared local summary and synchronization API. `GameTimeLibrary` provides a rebuildable local library index.
+
+- The local store is one SQLite database per title and save/cloud source, beside `DM_SAVE` in `playtime/<title>/<scope>.sqlite`. Transactions update changed segments and last activity. Retain the directory, including a recovery journal left by a crash, with play history when removing game files.
+- Existing local `<scope>.json` files are imported transactionally. An older built runtime can recreate one, so readers check for it before reusing a cached summary.
+- The chosen cloud folder carries `<title>/playtime.json`, independently of `save.dmsync`. Version 2 groups cumulative milliseconds by local Gregorian date and fresh segment UUID. Legacy `runs` remain undated. Merge uses the maximum per segment and the union of identities. `lastActivityAt` is a maximum UTC timestamp in milliseconds.
+- Call summaries off the UI thread. A snapshot includes its source fingerprint after database recovery and commit. A pending recovery journal invalidates cached fingerprints. Cloud JSON remains capped at 32 MiB, and local databases at 128 MiB.
+- `DMGameTimeSyncProgress` creates an attempt that can cancel pending cloud and local coordination. Pass it to `DMGameTimeSynchronizeSummaryWithProgress`. An accessor already executing must finish or roll back safely. A timeout is not a hard filesystem completion deadline.
+- Display runtime totals without adding independently observed launcher session durations. Keep legacy recency as a fallback, and never reconstruct duration from a last-played timestamp. These files are application-generated but not authenticated.
+
 ## Cheats
 
 Cheats are files, not settings: the host (`dm/host/hle_cheats.c`) reads Atmosphère-format cheat files for the running game's build and keeps the player's toggles beside them. A front end that wants to install cheats or pick which ones run, before the game starts, writes the same files; the Mac game window's Cheats menu and panel are one view of them. The engine itself and the format of a cheat line are described in [the cheats reference](reference/cheats.md); this section is the contract for the files.
